    <data name="AboutTitle" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>About Alder &amp; Salt</value>
    </data>
    <data name="AboutHeading" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>An old guesthouse, rebuilt in alder</value>
    </data>
    <data name="AboutDescription" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>The story of Alder &amp; Salt, a 32-room hotel in a rebuilt guesthouse from 1934 at Norddorf on Amrum, the people who run it and how to get here.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="AboutLede" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Freya Nielsen grew up on Amrum, and Jonas Brodersen cooked in Copenhagen for twelve years. In 2019 they bought the old Haus Seeblick in Norddorf and rebuilt it, room by room, in alder wood.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="StoryTitle" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Why alder, and why salt</value>
    </data>
    <data name="StoryBody1" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Alder is the tree of the island's only wood, behind the dunes at Norddorf. It's soft and warm and turns golden with the years, so we used it for every bed, door and table.</value>
    </data>
    <data name="StoryBody2" xml:space="preserve">
        <value>Salt is everything else: the wind, the sea on three sides, and the bread Jonas bakes every morning with sea salt from the Wadden coast. We kept the thatched roof and the front door from 1934, and added a sauna in the dunes.</value>
    </data>
